plan Rules
title   Rules on machines: invariants, turns and terminals, judged by every run and measured by rule coverage
status  proposed
goal
    An app author writes what must always hold beside the machine it is about, in the machine's own typed tokens, and
    Flow checks it everywhere it already runs the machine: every Story, generated run and sweep. Rule coverage shows
    which rules no run exercised, so an unexercised rule is never mistaken for a passed one. A rule that is about the
    graph, a terminal state, is proved by `flow check` and when a Story starts. Rules are claims, never guards: the core
    never calls one. They are optional, attached with a chained `.rules(build)`, and judged only by Story tooling; a
    Runtime **never reads them.** [decided: user 2026-11-22] Why: most values have no rules, and the Runtime judge, its
    switch and `RuleBroken` cost more than they protect. Reverse: the Runtime setup's `rules: "judge" | "off"` of the
    earlier text (@proposal.external-rules supersedes it).

    Today an app's own guarantees are hand-written checks inside single Stories (NUMBER-12's bounds filter), which the
    generator and sweeps never judge. Rules replace those with one declaration that every run judges.

    Every decision here is the user's (2026-11-19 and 2026-11-20). Not adopted, with why: `eventually` (Flow has no
    defined end of a run that ran its course; a Story may hold a call or leave a timer armed on purpose; a turns rule
    with `on` the turn that should clear a value covers most cases); `relations` across actors (it needs an actor-query
    API only to state a claim); `reachable` (the built-in unreachable-state warning and rule coverage cover it); a rule
    on where an action may be taken (drafted as `never`, `effects` and `allowed`; the word and the shape were not
    settled, and it is kept in @revisit.revisit); a `final` state that stops the actor (Flow actors' lifetimes belong to
    the host; subroutine exits already end a sub-flow); a `when` condition that coverage counts apart from the subject
    (kept simple; see @O5's limit).
outcomes
    O1  Rule kinds and what they read
        A machine declares rules with a chained `.rules(build)` on the value `machine()` returns; the builder receives
        the definition's `S` and `E` tokens and `turn`, so no rule names a state, event or operation by string. Rules
        are optional: a value with no `.rules(...)` has none, and **there is no `none`.** [decided: user 2026-11-22]
        Why: most values have no rules and a mandatory empty form edits every one. Reverse: require `.rules(...)` on
        every value a Story runs. This supersedes the earlier text, where rules were a `rules` block in the `machine()`
        callback, superseded by the user's 2026-11-22 decision (@proposal.external-rules). Two kinds are judged by runs:

        - `invariants`: true at every publication.
        - `turns`: true of every accepted turn, given the snapshot before and after it. Named turns, not transitions:
          the rule is judged on every accepted turn, including turns that only update memory, and "transition" already
          means a handler's `target`.

        An invariant entry is a function, or an object `{ check, in? }`; a turns entry is a bare function (no `event`),
        or `turn({ on, check })`, which types `event`. `in: S.X` and `on: E.X` name the rule's subject: a rule is
        exercised only when its subject comes up, and a rule without one at every publication or accepted turn. `in` on
        a compound holds in it and every state below it, as `S.X.is` means. `on: E.X` matches every accepted turn of X,
        whatever sent it: a user, an `onContext` reaction, a schedule, an operation ending or a host's raise into
        a fragment.

        A rule reads what a guard reads except `context`: `state`, `memory`, `props`, `operations` and `schedules`. Only
        turns rules also read `context`, as the turn's guards saw it (`before.context`). `before` is the last snapshot
        published before the turn, which may follow publications that were not turns. A consumer's context changes a
        publication before its own state and memory react, so a rule comparing context with a memory copy of it breaks
        on correct code during that lag; a rule over context agrees only with guards that read context directly. A turns
        rule written `turn({ on: E.X, check })` also gets `event`, typed as X's payload; the bare function has no
        `event`. `turn` comes from the rules builder, not the machine body (supersedes Types Q3, "`turn` a body token").
        **`turn` from the builder** [decided: user 2026-11-22] Why: a token's memory type comes from the definition,
        which `.rules` has, and the body no longer holds rules. Reverse: `turn` back as a body token.

        Only accepted turns are judged by turns rules and counted for them. A denied or unhandled turn runs no handler,
        but it may still publish a lane change (@spec.Turn.R19): invariants judge that publication, turns rules skip it.
        A restore publishes but is not a turn, so invariants judge the restored snapshot and turns rules skip it; a pair
        across a reload is never judged, because saves coalesce and the last captured snapshot may be older than the
        last published one.

        ```ts
        // existing: definition, machine, S, E, snapped (stepping.ts)
        // NEW: .rules (chained on what machine() returns), invariants, turns, turn, in, on, check, event
        // moved: turn (from the machine body's tokens to the rules builder's)
        // user-defined: StartedWhileHolding, SetLandsInBounds, CommitMovesByOne, CommitDiffersFromStart
        export const StepperMachine = machine(Stepper, ({ S, E }) => ({
          default: S.IDLE,
          on: {/* handlers, unchanged */},
          states: {/* states, unchanged */},
        })).rules(({ S, E, turn }) => ({
          invariants: {
            StartedWhileHolding: { in: S.HOLD_REPEAT, check: ({ memory }) => memory.started !== null },
          },
          turns: {
            SetLandsInBounds: turn({
              on: E.Set,
              check: ({ event, before, after }) =>
                after.memory.value === (event.value === null ? null : snapped(before.memory.settings, event.value)),
            }),
            // Every commit, whichever event made it (Release, Cancel, Blur, Key, WheelDue): the bare form, no `on`.
            CommitMovesByOne: ({ before, after }) =>
              after.memory.lastCommit.seq === before.memory.lastCommit.seq ||
              after.memory.lastCommit.seq === before.memory.lastCommit.seq + 1,
            CommitDiffersFromStart: ({ before, after }) =>
              after.memory.lastCommit.seq === before.memory.lastCommit.seq ||
              after.memory.lastCommit.value !== (before.memory.started?.from ?? before.memory.value),
          },
        }))

        // context, read by a turns rule, where Begin's guard reads `context.count` itself
        CartNotEmptyOnBegin: turn({ on: E.Begin, check: ({ before }) => before.context.count > 0 }),
        ```

        A value range such as "value stays within min and max" is not an invariant of this Stepper: `Configure` may
        narrow the bounds without re-clamping `value`. `SetLandsInBounds` states what is true instead.
    O2  Terminals
        `terminals` lists state tokens, leaves or compounds at any depth, that no edge leaves. A leaf allows only a
        self-transition; a compound allows moves inside its subtree. Handlers on ancestors count, and in a subroutine so
        do its exits. Every event source counts: user events, `onContext` reactions, schedules and late operation
        endings. Restarts are not edges: a new incarnation, a restore or a trait's `resetOn`. A terminal pins the state
        only; memory may still change, and a turns rule claims more.

        `flow check` proves terminals over the compiled graph: an edge out is a `TerminalLeft` error naming the handler
        and the state it can leave; an empty list or a state listed twice is refused; a listed state is never warned as
        likely stuck. Guard functions are opaque, so the proof counts any handler that may fire in the terminal; a
        state-token guard (`guard: [S.IDLE]`) narrows where its branch fires, and the error names the handler so the
        author can restructure it. Being about the graph, terminals are proved by `flow check` and again when a Story
        starts (memoised per value; a `TerminalLeft` fails the Story's verdict, in `storyCompiled`); runs never judge
        them, so they have no token and the switch (@O4, `off: "all"` included) **does not apply to them.** [decided:
        user 2026-11-22] Why: a graph proof covers every run, so a run-time judge could only repeat it, and a value only
        a test runs is otherwise never proved. Reverse: `flow check` only, as the earlier text of this paragraph had it
        (superseded by the user's 2026-11-22 decision, @proposal.external-rules). See @spec.Types.Q6.

        Superseded (the earlier O2 text, by the decision above): "terminals are proved by `flow check` only, never
        judged by runs; they have no token, `RuleBroken` never names one, and neither the switch (@O4, `off: "all"`
        included) nor the Runtime's `rules` option (@O6) applies to them." Why it stood: a graph proof covers every run.

        Terminals live in the machine's `.rules(...)`, not on the definition's states list: a terminal is a claim, not
        shape, and changes no behaviour. Flow does not stop an actor in a terminal state.

        ```ts
        // states: ["PENDING", { SHIPPED: ["IN_TRANSIT", "DELIVERED"] }, "REFUNDED", "CANCELLED"]
        OrderMachine = machine(Order, body).rules(({ S }) => ({
          terminals: [S.REFUNDED, S.CANCELLED, S.SHIPPED.DELIVERED],   // NEW: terminals
        }))
        ```
    O3  Rule names and tokens
        Rules have their own namespace under `rules`, apart from the shared namespace of states, memory keys,
        operations, props, emits and trait instances. A name is unique across `invariants` and `turns` in one machine or
        fragment: a duplicate, inside one object or across the two, is a type error and also refused at construction
        (`RuleNameTwice`), which catches casts and plain JavaScript. Each rule is a typed token, `machine.rules.<Name>`
        or `fragment.rules.<Name>`, and the token carries the definition id (`Shop/Order.PaidHasReceipt`).

        `machine()` is unchanged: it returns `Machine<D>`, and `.rules(build)` returns `Machine<D, N>`, where `N` is the
        rule names read from the keys of the builder's result alone. Nothing captures the body or the checks.
        `Machine<D, N>` assigns to `Machine<D>`, and a `Machine<D>` annotation loses the tokens, so such code still
        compiles and reaches a rule by name only where the type is kept. A shared build is typed by `rules(Definition,
        build)`, which returns the build itself. An unknown key (`chek`, `terminalz`) is refused at construction and by
        `flow check`, never by types (@spec.Types.Q19). This replaces the earlier text, where `machine()` took the
        body's type as a second parameter, superseded by the user's 2026-11-22 decision (@proposal.external-rules). The
        build repeats the type spike in `tests/types/` against the real `machine()`, `trait()` and `subroutine()`.
    O4  Judging in tests
        Every Story, generated run, reload sweep and pair sweep judges every rule with no extra call. A broken rule
        fails the run with the shortest Story that shows it (the generator's shrink), naming the rule, the snapshot
        before and after, and for a fragment copy the copy. When several rules break at one publication or turn, each
        gets its own record, invariants first and then in declaration order; the run fails on the first and the report
        lists every rule broken there.

        Checking is switchable and never silent. A Story's chained `.rules({ expectBroken, off })` holds for the whole
        Story wherever it is written, so Story helpers can add it; it is kept in the Story's plan as data beside its
        options, not as a step. `expectBroken` passes only if each named rule breaks at least once; a named rule never
        exercised fails as "never broke". After a rule's first break the run goes on, later breaks are not failures, and
        generated runs started from the Story treat it as off. `off` (a list of tokens, or `"all"`) is not judged and
        does not count as exercised. A rule in both lists is refused. Rule coverage lists every rule expected or off.
        Generated runs and sweeps started from a Story inherit its switch; reload-sweep forks drop `expectBroken` and
        still judge every rule (@spec.Host).

        ```ts
        // NEW: .rules, expectBroken, off, machine.rules.<Name>; renamed: runRules (the run rules option)
        // A planted fault: a Stepper variant whose Set skips the clamp; it shares the original's build, so the token
        // is the same rule.
        export const UnclampedStepperMachine = machine(Stepper, unclampedBody).rules(stepperRules)
        export const setUnclamped = Story.machine(UnclampedStepperMachine, {
          id: "number-input/set-unclamped",
          runRules,
        })
          .rules({ expectBroken: [UnclampedStepperMachine.rules.SetLandsInBounds] })
          .send(Stepper.E.Configure({ settings: bounded }))
          .send(Stepper.E.Set({ value: 99 }))

        export const stressEdge = numberStory("number-input/stress-edge")
          .rules({ off: "all" })
          .send(quantity, Stepper.E.Configure({ settings: bounded }))
        ```

        Flow's own run rules (the runtime's guarantees, which no app can break and no machine declares) get the same
        three modes only in Flow's own test helper, named by the rule value, never by a string: `checked(run, { off:
        [everyCommitEndsOnce] })`. They are never public tokens and never in a Story's `.rules`. Their `breaches` and
        `breachesOf` are renamed `broken` and `brokenOf`, so one word, broken, is used everywhere.
    O5  Rule coverage
        The test run reports how often each rule was exercised and how many of all rules were, like code coverage. A
        rule no run exercised is listed as unseen, with why (its state never reached, its event never accepted, its
        fragment never placed), never as passed. Unseen is a measurement, not a failure: it never fails the suite, and
        nothing marks a rule as allowed to be unseen. Rules that are `off` are left out of the total; expected rules are
        counted and listed as expected. A fragment rule is one row per placement, its copies added together, named
        beside the rule's id (the id carries the definition, not the placement). Every value with no rules is listed
        too, as information. Rule coverage is computed by the run and never stored, so `flow check` does not show it. It
        is its own report, apart from Story coverage (`story list --coverage`) and Inspection's unseen.

        ```text
        Rule coverage  12 of 14 exercised (86%)
          Number/Stepper.SetLandsInBounds     checked  seen 214 (on Set)
          Number/Stepper.CommitMovesByOne     checked  seen 1,930 turns
          Ui/Upload.CancelSticks at Desk/Editor/uploads    checked  seen 6 (copies a, b, c)
          unseen  Number/Stepper.StartedWhileHolding    in HOLD_REPEAT, never reached
          unseen  Shop/Pay/card.NumberBeforeCheck       CardEntry never placed
          no rules  Shop/Pay
        ```

        The limit: a rule counts its subject, not the condition inside its check. "If cancelled, it stays cancelled"
        counts every turn of its subject even when nothing was ever cancelled. The exact format is settled in the build.
    O6  Outside tests
        Only Story tooling judges rules: Stories and everything built from them (generated runs, sweeps,
        `propertyTest`). A Runtime and `app()` never read rules, so there is no Runtime switch, no `rules` setup option,
        no `enabled` flag, no live judge and no `RuleBroken` evidence kind; rules ship in the bundle and never run
        **outside a Story.** [decided: user 2026-11-22] Why: most values have no rules and the Runtime judge costs a
        switch, a `last` map and a redaction for a claim a test already makes. Reverse: the Runtime setup's `rules:
        "judge" | "off"` with `RuleBroken` evidence. This supersedes the earlier O6 text (rules inline in `machine()`,
        the Runtime's `rules: "judge" | "off"`, `RuleBroken` redaction and a build-time constant), superseded by the
        user's 2026-11-22 decision (@proposal.external-rules); the 2026-11-22 `rules` setup option ruling
        (@spec.Types.Q5) is superseded with it, and its `enabled` overturn stands. The run rules are one list in one
        module that the config and `Story.app` both import (@spec.Host).

        Superseded (the earlier O6, by the decision above, @proposal.external-rules): "Rules stay inline in `machine()`
        ... The Runtime setup chooses `rules: "judge" | "off"`, read once at the host edge from the app's own
        environment; **Stories and the CLI always judge.**" [decided: user 2026-11-22, now superseded] Why it stood: a
        flag read in a rule's body is evaluated at import, where the plain CLI has no `import.meta.env`. With `judge`,
        the host judged after each publication or accepted turn, kept the previous snapshot as `before`, and recorded a
        broken rule as `RuleBroken` evidence with `Flow.private` memory redacted; the core never called a rule. Reverse
        of the supersession: restore that Runtime option.

        **The case that remains is rules written while the bare value is the one that runs.** [decided: agent specs lead
        2026-11-22] It is closed without making rules mandatory: a run holding two values of one id with different rules
        is refused (`RulesDiffer`); a lint rule stops a module that calls `X.rules(...)` from exporting `X`
        (`no-bare-export`), and another accepts only an arrow or an identifier as the argument (`rules-argument`); rule
        coverage and `flow explain` list every value with no rules; and `flow check` warns `RulesBypassed` (a warning
        only) when an app or Story runs a bare value of an id some loaded value has ruled. A shared build typed
        `rules(Order, build)` never needs the bare value exported. Every Story route starts through one function,
        `storyCompiled`, which also proves each ruled value's terminals. Why: rules are optional, so the bare value must
        not slip past unnoticed. Reverse: make rules mandatory.
    O7  Fragments
        A `trait` or `subroutine` value takes the same chained `.rules(build)` as `machine()`: a trait's builder gets
        `E` and `turn` (no `S`, `terminals` or `in`), a subroutine's gets its own `S`, `E` and `turn`. The definition
        places the value, so placing the fragment brings its rules and a host can never remove or weaken one
        (`RulesDiffer` and `flow check` walk placements). A fragment's rules read only what the fragment declares: its
        own memory, operations and schedules, and for a subroutine its own states (a trait has none). Never props,
        context or the host's data, which a fragment cannot declare or see. Subjects are its own `S` and `E` tokens,
        `hostEvents` included; `on` matches a host's raise into the fragment.

        Rules are judged while the incarnation is live, on each placement and each `.each` copy separately; a frozen
        `.resumable()` trait is not judged. Turns rules skip a copy's entry, reset (`resetOn`) and exit, as they skip a
        restore: there is no before or after of the same copy. `Story.fragment` judges them alone against its stand-in
        host; host Stories judge them where placed. Rule coverage names the placement (`at Desk/Editor/uploads`), and a
        broken rule also names the copy (`uploads[a]`). A fragment token (`Upload.rules.CancelSticks`) carries the trait
        id (`Ui/Upload.CancelSticks`), not a placement, and switches every placement. Built with fragments
        and `Story.fragment`.

        ```ts
        // existing (Plan shape): trait, definition, machine, Flow.input, Flow.initial, .each, raise,
        //   Story.fragment, enter, leave, Story.machine
        // NEW: .rules on a trait (E and turn; no S)
        // user-defined: Upload, CancelSticks, RefNeverChanges, Editor, EditorMachine, Attach, CancelAll,
        //   attachments, uploads, uploadAlone, twoUploads
        export const Upload = trait(
          {
            id: "Upload",
            events: { Cancel: {} },
            memory: {
              ref: Flow.input(Schema.String),
              cancelled: Flow.initial(Schema.Boolean, false),
            },
          },
          () => ({
            on: { Cancel: { target: "self", updateMemory: () => ({ cancelled: true }) } },
          }),
        ).rules(() => ({
          turns: {
            CancelSticks: ({ before, after }) => !before.memory.cancelled || after.memory.cancelled,
            RefNeverChanges: ({ before, after }) => before.memory.ref === after.memory.ref,
          },
        }))

        export const Editor = definition({
          id: "Desk/Editor",
          states: ["EDITING"],
          events: { Attach: { ref: Schema.String }, CancelAll: {} },
          memory: {
            attachments: Flow.initial(Schema.Array(Schema.Struct({ ref: Schema.String })), []),
          },
          traits: { uploads: Upload.each({ of: "attachments", by: "ref", max: 10 }) },
        })

        export const EditorMachine = machine(Editor, ({ S, E }) => ({
          default: S.EDITING,
          on: {
            Attach: {
              updateMemory: ({ event, memory }) => ({ attachments: [...memory.attachments, { ref: event.ref }] }),
            },
            CancelAll: { raise: [E.uploads.Cancel] },
          },
        }))

        // The fragment alone: Upload.CancelSticks and Upload.RefNeverChanges, seen 2 each.
        export const uploadAlone = Story.fragment(Upload, { id: "upload/alone", runRules })
          .enter({ ref: "file-1" })
          .send(Upload.E.Cancel({}))
          .send(Upload.E.Cancel({}))
          .leave()

        // The host: Desk/Editor/uploads.CancelSticks and .RefNeverChanges, seen 2 each (copies a, b, one raise each).
        export const twoUploads = Story.machine(EditorMachine, { id: "editor/two-uploads", runRules })
          .send(Editor.E.Attach({ ref: "a" }))
          .send(Editor.E.Attach({ ref: "b" }))
          .send(Editor.E.CancelAll({}))
        ```
    O8  Tools
        `flow explain` lists each machine's rules and every value with no rules. `flow check` proves terminals (@O2),
        warns `RulesBypassed` (an app or Story runs a bare value whose ruled form it also loads; an agent call, @O6),
        refuses an unknown key in a rules object, and warns `RuleSubjectUnreachable` when the compiled graph cannot
        reach a rule's `in` state or no reachable state handles its `on` event. Guards make the graph an
        over-approximation, so that warning is never false; it is a warning, not an error, so a machine mid-edit still
        checks, and it separates "impossible" from "not tested yet", which rule coverage alone cannot. The test reporter
        prints rule coverage (@O5).
verification
    V1  Type tests prove rule tokens, inputs and subjects against the real `machine()`: `machine.rules.<Name>` exists
        for each rule and not for others; a duplicate name, in one kind or across both, is a type error and
        `RuleNameTwice` at construction; `event` is typed from `on` and absent without it; `in` on a turns rule and `on`
        on an invariant are errors; `context` is readable in turns rules only; each check's input is inferred; `.rules`
        twice and a wrong definition's build are errors; a shared `rules(Def, build)` and a `Machine<typeof X>`
        annotation still compile; the trait and subroutine forms hold on the real types.
    V2  Stories prove every run judges: a planted broken invariant and a planted broken turns rule each fail a Story,
        a generated run (with its shortest Story) and the reload sweep; denied and unhandled turns are not judged by
        turns rules while their lane changes are judged by invariants; a restore is judged by invariants only; several
        rules broken at once each get a record, in order.
    V3  Stories prove the switch: `expectBroken` passes only when the rule breaks and fails as "never broke" when it
        is never exercised, `off` skips and is listed, a rule in both lists is refused, and a Story's switch reaches the
        runs generated from it.
    V4  Tests prove rule coverage: counts per rule and subject, unseen with its reason, `off` left out of the total,
        and that unseen never fails.
    V5  `flow check` tests prove terminals (an ancestor handler, a schedule, a late ending and a subroutine exit each 
         give
        `TerminalLeft`; restarts, self-transitions and a branch whose state-token guard excludes the terminal do not; an
        empty or duplicate list is refused) and `RuleSubjectUnreachable`; `flow explain` lists rules.
    V6  Tests prove nothing judges outside a Story: a plain Runtime test with a broken rule passes, `RulesDiffer`
        refuses a bare and a ruled value of one id in a run, `RulesEmpty` and `RulesWrongSubject` refuse at
        construction, terminals are proved at Story start, `flow check` warns `RulesBypassed`, the lint rules
        `no-bare-export` and `rules-argument` fire, and the core calls no rule.
    V7  NUMBER-12's hand check and at least one other become declared rules; each rule kind and terminals has a
        planted fault caught by `nub run faults --only`; `.rules()` refuses a duplicate rule name passed past the types;
        the run rules' `broken` rename is complete.
    V8  With fragments: per-copy judging, turns rules skipping a copy's entry, reset and exit, `on` matching a host's
        raise, coverage by placement, `Story.fragment` judging alone, and a host unable to remove a fragment's
        rule (`RulesDiffer`).
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        The suite and bench cost of rules, measured with rules on and off before a budget is set. Whether an operation
        row is busy in the same publication as the commit that started it, which rules over `operations` depend on.
        Switching one fragment placement on its own, as a later extension. The rule coverage format.
